this is a production of cornell  university  thank you  great crowd so happy to be here this is  actually my fourth trip to new york this  year as national teacher of the year  i've been here all day at the university  spoke this morning actually at ithaca  college with ithaca college faculty and  also faculty from cornell this afternoon  had a chance to speak with education  majors from cornell and i'm here with  you tonight to give you a little bit  more broad perspective not just about my  background personally but what i've been  doing this year's national teacher of  the year and what the conversations are  that are going on in america right now  related to public education and sort of  how we can all be a part of that so i  really appreciate you coming out tonight  and i want to start by telling you just  a little bit about myself as was just  mentioned um i did go to princeton  university and  growing up i thought i always wanted to  be a teacher my mother's a retired  elementary school teacher and in general  i thought being a teacher would be a  really great thing to be  that career goal was sort of received in  a lukewarm fashion until i decided to  enroll at princeton university and then  the conversation really changed and then  the conversation became well you know  those who can do and those who can't  teach  and i was asked when i got to princeton  well  you know you came to princeton and all  you want to be is a teacher  and what a waste  and i know that some people here at  cornell can relate to that uh philosophy  and dr duff actually sitting up front  here is a fellow princetonian we  actually graduated from each other one  year apart and he thought it would be  nice for me to come here and kind of  share my perspective on how and why i  got into teaching despite  considerable social pressure not to do  so but it really all goes back to  this journey here i did start  volunteering  i decided when i was at princeton that  okay well maybe i should be something  more so i decided to be pre-med  and in general that career goal was very  well received so everybody else was  happy and i was plodding along planning  to be pre-med took the mcats did the  whole nine yards and i thought to myself  well you know if i'm not going to do  teaching as a career then by golly i'm  going to go volunteer  and i found myself in this classroom at  the new jersey school for the deaf  with these students who changed my life  they changed my life and people ask me  they said well you know what was it  about that environment what was it about  being with those students that made you  completely scrap your pre-med plans and  decide to go into teaching and for  anybody who's ever been in a classroom  it's easy to understand because there's  this energy  there's this interaction knowing that  you're making a difference every day i  just really wanted to be a part of that  and so i came from this environment  to where i am now  and this is a snapshot from my classroom  at urbana high school in frederick  county maryland  i've been teaching at the school  approximately a total of about 11 years  and i love this picture and i love to  tell people what i see in this picture i  look at this picture i see progress  because i see roughly an equal mix of  male and female students  and remember this is an ap chemistry  class  and that is not always the way it used  to be we are starting to close the  gender gap in some of our advanced  science subjects particularly in  chemistry and biology we have more work  to do in physics and math and  engineering but we're making progress i  look at the picture and i see some  diversity but not enough  because the diversity of my own  classroom still does not reflect the  diversity of my school and that's a  problem and our work is not finished  until it does  but students in my class who have 504  plans or ieps which means they have some  form of special needs over the years  i've accommodated students in my  classroom with everything from  asperger's syndrome to dyslexia  dysgraphia low vision and people tell me  sometimes that those students really  have no business being in my class  because it's an ap chemistry class  and that should mean something  and perhaps my expectations just aren't  high enough  i also look at this picture and i see at  least one face that's missing and it's  because that student is at the career  and technology center the vocational  student and somebody asked me well why  would a student in the vocational  program be in your ap chemistry class  that's a conflict  and my question is why is there only one  i mean with all the overlap between  taking knowledge and applying it which  is what i do in my chemistry classes  what they do in the vocational program  and aren't we really all teaching the  same things that we should be able to  make some more progress there  so in between in the middle of my career  i had the opportunity to teach at the  maryland school for the deaf and this is  a snapshot of a group of students that i  taught while i was there maryland school  for the deaf is a very interesting place  even though that maryland is a small  state it's a very diverse state and this  school draws students from all over the  state so students from our inner cities  students from our eastern shore the  beach areas students from the mountains  and everything in between and not just  students from different socioeconomic  backgrounds but students who have  different varying levels of hearing loss  so for example i taught some students  here who were born deaf and grew up with  families using american sign language i  taught other students who were not  diagnosed with their deafness till much  later in life so they came to me at the  high school level maybe not knowing any  sign language at all and of course at  this particular school all of the  instruction was through american sign  language we didn't use voice at all so  sometimes in my chemistry physics class  i would have a student who maybe was  growing up with spoken spanish in the  home had no idea about asl but yet we're  in my chemistry and physics class trying  to learn through asl so it's a very  complicated dynamic situation  so basically you know i've been hanging  out the better part of the past 15 years  you know teaching everybody's favorite  subject  and i can see that that's true here by  the show of hands about how many of you  this is really favorite subject and of  course i'm just kidding obviously  chemistry is not everybody's favorite  subject  but as educators as a teacher it's my  job to somehow bring this to everybody  and usually when people hear chemistry  they think of one of two things they  either think about the facts and the  formulas that they despise so much  or they think about the fun stuff they  think about the fun stuff and yeah  there's fun stuff in chemistry you know  my students love to do lab activities  and of course i try to engage them in  everything that i can when students come  into my classroom really every day they  want to know two things right they have  two questions for me first they want to  know  are we going to burn something today  that's number one and if we're not going  to burn something the question is are we  going to  blow something up  right that's what students want to do in  a chemistry class and so yes as a  teacher trying to engage my students i  try to bring them in any way that i can  i know that most of my students are  really into the harry potter series  and they've seen the movies and they see  that big green flu network do you know  what i'm talking about if you've seen  the movie and they know that's chemistry  and they say oh that's so cool you know  can we do that in your class and i say  sure we can do that and we set up little  green flu networks all over the counter  tops and then they see how they burn  green and they say oh you know can we  make them red and i say yeah we can do  that you know and then we turn them red  and then they want to know why and then  you can actually teach them something i  have students in my class who swear up  and down that they are not science  people you know i am not a science  person you are not going to turn me into  a science person they say i am a history  person and i said well that's fine you  know there's a lot of science in history  we talk about things like the hindenburg  explosion and all of my history buffs  can tell me all about that and they've  seen the video and they've read the  theories and then they say yeah that was  a really amazing explosion and then they  make the connection and there's the  explosion can we do that can we do that  in your classroom and i say yeah sure we  can do that we can take balloons and we  can fill them up with hydrogen gas and  in a carefully controlled situation  we can explode those little mini  hydrogen balloons so they can get a  sense for what that history was like  i've never met a student from anywhere  who doesn't want to set off an alcohol  cannon  it's just one of those universally  appealing things  and as teach as a teacher i would be  crazy not to tap into that but you know  it's not all about fun and games and  entertainment it's really so that our  students wind up with a serious look on  their face so that they get engaged  better yet to have this look of pride on  their face and i love this particular  picture because rochelle's lab work is  reflected in her goggles with that sense  of pride of having mastered something  really truly difficult and with  everything that we know about data these  days and there's a lot of talk in  education about data  data action research collect the data  what does it mean and that's all  important but i've learned over the  years and having starting in deaf  education that i get just as much  information out of looking at students  faces and that's something that i've  done all along but then last may 3rd i  found myself in a very unlikely place  for a public school teacher  with the president of the united states  to my right and the secretary of  education to my left standing with  the 55 state and territorial teachers of  the year  and knowing in that moment that i was no  longer a chemistry teacher hanging out  in my classroom blowing up stuff and  doing cool labs with my students  that i was now a spokesperson for public  education representing all teachers  early childhood teachers elementary  school teachers middle school teachers  high school teachers from all subject  areas and it would be my job for a year  to find the themes that connect us all  now as a little time out you might be  thinking  how exactly do you wind up in that place  i mean really how would you get chosen  for something like that what is the  process and i'll just make a long story  very very short but it actually started  at my school level the faculty that i  worked with in my school nominated me  put me on the ballot to apply for  teacher of the year and i told him i  didn't want to do it  i said i want to do it because it would  take too much time  away from my students because with any  process like this it's a process there's  a lot of essay writing there are  face-to-face interviews  and all the while you never think that  you're going to go very far anyway so i  did start the process because i was very  honored to be to be nominated and then i  was named teacher of the year at my  county level in frederick county and  maryland and then all of the county  teachers went to the state level and  then i went through another series of  evaluations at the state level and then  i was named the maryland teacher of the  year  and then once you're named your state  teacher of the year then all of those  application materials get sent to the  national committee and at that point the  game changes a little bit because at  that point if you're your state teacher  of the year it's pretty much a given  that everybody's fabulous in the  classroom everybody's doing amazing  things in the classroom and so from that  pool of 55 four finalists were selected  of which i was one we were all brought  into washington dc and in washington dc  we were put through a series of  evaluations that would simulate what we  would do as national teacher of the year  we had a classic interview where we had  to answer questions  we had to do a mock tv interview like a  good morning america we had to do a  press conference i had to do a keynote  speech we had to do a working networking  dinner  so different situations that would  evaluate our ability to be a  spokesperson which is what this year is  all about  people look at this picture and they  think that i have a direct line to the  president and the secretary of education  i do not  i do not everything that i do is by  invitation  and it's not a role where you are seated  with the policy makers that's not the  purpose of the year though i have had  some opportunities to interact with them  both that's certainly not the focus but  i have as i like to say  just been teaching in a larger classroom  i mean i've told myself all along that  the key word in national teacher of the  year is teacher you know i'm still a  teacher i'm always a teacher i've always  considered myself a teacher but i have a  much bigger classroom much bigger  audience this year if you look at the  map  everything that's shaded in hot pink  shows you where i have been as national  teacher of the year and many of those  states multiple visits i mean four  visits to new york three to texas three  to mississippi um history people please  forgive me geography folks but yes china  and japan floating around there  aimlessly in the ocean i have been to  china and japan obviously the map is not  to scale  also heading to singapore at the end of  may so that'll be three international  trips on my schedule you see that  arkansas is shaded orange instead of