e teacher and  national teacher has learned that if you  have something to say to the media you  better say it quickly make your point  because otherwise the conversation is  going to change very quickly i love this  one this next question is yes yes yes i  understand that there are so many  challenges in education but  if you had a magic wand  i love that if you had a magic wand  and i got to a certain point where i had  been asked that question so many times  that i just flat out said in an  interview i don't have a magic wand no  one does  i mean that's the truthful answer  that is the truthful answer  it was on public television in fact  nobody has a magic wand  it's just not that simple and i know  that here in america we like that quick  fix we want that easy answer we want to  try to pretend that there's just one  thing that could make everything right  despite the diversity of our nation and  it's not just true but i understand that  these questions are all designed to get  at what the real issue is and the real  issue is how do we reach all students  that's what really weighs on everybody's  mind is the equity issue we we still  admit that in our country there are  pockets of excellence that's for sure  and then there are pockets of great need  and that there are huge disparities in  access and resources for students  sometimes the disparity is separated by  just a matter of miles  and and everybody's trying to figure out  why that continues and what we can do  about it but but it's it's a good  natured question i mean how do we reach  all students and you know now that i'm a  parent myself  this question is very very important to  me this is my little girl carly  and she's sitting with the boy she says  she's going to marry  and they are at their preschool  graduation  yeah their preschool graduation  and i noticed that some people in the  audience said oh like it's not cute and  that's great and some people laugh when  i say that  they're like preschool graduation like  are you serious like how silly to which  i say you know is it really that silly  is it really that silly if we're going  for equity here that from the youngest  possible age to have absolutely every  child wear that cap and gown and hold  that piece of paper so that they will  believe  that they too will be what we're calling  these days and you've probably heard the  term college and career ready  and i put that in quotes because we're  not going to debate it at the moment  but the point is that if we think about  our own children or a child very close  to us in our lives we know exactly what  that means  i know what it means for my daughter to  be college ready career ready life ready  world ready i know everything i want her  to be in my mind i don't need to see it  spelled out on a checklist i don't need  it to be in a manual or a curriculum or  a set of standards i know  what that is  and you know her little boyfriend i  don't know him very well  but  it's my job as an educator  to want for other people's children  what i want for my own  and as we work towards this equity that  we all desire so much i think if we can  just keep that very simple fact in mind  that moves us a huge step forward  so just to give you an idea of how that  conversation is playing out over the  country i'm going to take a week from my  schedule as national teacher and just  show you the different conversations  that i'm involved in and i can't  necessarily say that this week is  typical but it'll give you an idea of  some of the things that i've been doing  if we flash back to november um first i  was involved in a conference week  regarding educator effectiveness big  deal right now is about teachers and how  we're going to evaluate them and what  makes an effective teacher and what  makes an exceptional teacher and how do  we spell that all out in a form that's  somehow fair and perhaps could be linked  to compensation that's a big issue so my  day on monday november 14th started with  that issue and then i went down to  washington dc for congressional briefing  where we were talking about the ap  chemistry redesign curriculum is a big  issue in the in the discussion uh in the  country right now what are we going to  teach kids and even something uh with a  long strand standing tradition like the  ap programs they're even looking at  their curriculum and redesigning it and  trying to make it less facts more  application less memorization more  inquiry so the tests are changing  everything's changing really effective  immediately so i was weighing in on that  then i went to a women's heritage center  for a stem exhibit again equity is still  on everybody's minds we're trying to  figure out how do we get more women into  stem science technology engineering  mathematics how do we get more  individuals from minority backgrounds  into stem and so any event that kind of  highlights that is something that i  would be involved in you know we need to  celebrate what's going on in our public  schools so that we know what to  replicate and to emulate you turn on the  news on any given night and you hear a  lot of things that we're not doing right  in the public schools i mean that  dominates the conversation but there are  a lot of celebrations that go on and  it's it's very important because  otherwise we don't know what's truly  working so i found myself at the blue  ribbon schools  ceremony  with arne duncan celebrating great  things happening and then i found myself  later that day again celebrating  american education week because it's  really important that we get these  positive stories out in the news  i mean i i suggested in my national  teacher of the application i mean  imagine if the national news began each  evening with a segment titled highlights  in american public education  that actually celebrate what's really  working that's really going on that's  good in our public schools  not only would we be celebrating it but  we would know what to emulate and what  to replicate we don't know enough about  the great things that are really going  on as i travel the country people say oh  michelle have you heard about this going  on here have you heard about this  program and i say yeah no i i really  haven't  because we don't get that message out  there nearly enough so that's obviously  an issue of concern the next day i found  myself with students i found myself with  a little people  at a local elementary school i was  speaking to kindergartners first graders  all the way up to fifth graders and  their parents and what i found really  interesting at that student assembly and  you know there's a different situation  for me because remember i'm a high  school teacher but you know they  corralled them all into the cafeteria  like you probably remember when you were  in elementary school and everybody was  sitting in nice neat rows just like  their teachers told them with their legs  folded you know crisscross applesauce  and all that  and  they're all looking up at me  and you know when i grabbed the mic that  day  i actually hesitated for a minute before  i started speaking because i thought to  myself man  you know i better be good  because i've got the future sitting  there staring back at me  and whatever i say is going to stick in  their brains and i better be good  and i asked them some questions that day  now i'm not full enough to open up a  question to you know 500 kindergarteners  and have them all call out and screaming  but i taught him a little bit of sign  language i told him yes and i taught him  no and i asked the kindergartners i  asked them if they thought i looked like  a scientist  and over half of them said no  now tell me why  a five-year-old  would would tell me that i don't look  like a scientist i mean on what basis is  it because i'm a woman is it because i  have long hair and it's kind of curly  how do these images and these  perceptions gets ingrained in our  children so early  and little kids are very honest so  they'll tell you that so we really have  some work to do in our country with  perceptions starting from our very young  children and believing you know who  could be a scientist who could be a  college graduate you know who could be a  professional  you know we need to do a lot with  perceptions later that day i found  myself with my own little girl  at her parent day at her school and then  i caught a plane to go to phoenix and in  phoenix i went to a policy conference  when i go to a policy conference i'm  usually the only teacher there  i am the  teacher voice  the teacher voice  and in that particular situation at this  particular event we went on a tour of a  vocational school you know we have a lot  of students in our country who are on a  vocational track this is just a fact and  we have to serve their needs we also  have to understand our vocational  students because vocational students  also have college plans i got news for  you and often they have very big goals  and they have it all figured out because  they explain it to me they say yeah i'm  going to graduate from high school i'm  going to get this job i'm going to save  money then i'm going to go to college  then i'm going to go back and start my  own business so  it's very unfair of us  to put people on tracks and say well  you're either vocational or you're  academic because there's so much  crossover so i'd have a chance to go to  that school or chat on educator  effectiveness a little bit of networking  and then finally i was done  giving my own keynote and you might  think about this week that i just  described and somebody asked me once  they said what do you do when you get  sick  it's just not on the schedule it's just  it just doesn't happen you just um  fortunately i've had a very good year  but i've not gotten sick but that  particular week i got to go home on  sunday and you say well that's nice you  get at least you get to get home every  sunday no i don't get to go home every  sunday it just happened that that sunday  i got to go home and i've appreciated  everybody who's asked about my family  obviously this is a tremendous sacrifice  for my little girl and for my husband  and  people ask if my family can travel with  me and they really can't because they  have their lives in maryland you know my  little girl's in school she's in first  grade you know she's working on her art  she's working on her public speaking  she's working on her little science  experiments  there's my husband who's currently  winning the husband of the year award  as i travel 80 90 percent of the time  and there was one time in november or  december where they did get to travel  with me to arizona  nbc was having a big celebration of  education it had happened to be at an  nfl event we had never been to an nfl  game before so this was a quick two day  trip of course we got all the gear and  and we do wear that gear back in  maryland even though we are in steelers  and our ravens territory we we boldly  wear that cardinals here back at home so  every now and then um you know they  might be able to join me for something  but usually i'm off on my own i spend a  lot of time at universities and colleges  cornell is obviously not my first i've  done a lot of colleges and universities  over the course of the year and i see a  lot of interesting things going on a lot  of the conversation is the same how do  we best prepare our teacher candidates  last week i was at ole miss  and they took me to a room  where i saw this on the screen  has anybody seen this before  i thought wow what is this this is  something called teach live that  actually came out of the university of  central florida  and basically it's a classroom an empty  classroom with a screen  with  digital avatar virtual children  and these children on the screen can be  programmed  to have different personalities  different learning challenges  and the idea is that a teacher in  training  will step into that room with the  students on the screen  and practice teaching them  before they get in front of live  children  this is new to me  i just saw this last week  i don't know what i think about it  because i'm still trying to reflect and  process it but as i say when i travel  not only am i sharing things from my own  perspective but i'm learning about the  different things that are going on and i  tell you things are really rapidly  changing in education  i was at manhattanville college in  purchase new york talking with teacher  candidates there went to kent state ohio  talked with then i came back to new york  for a stem panel and again this is  another situation where i am the only  teacher on the panel and then a little  bit more with career tech and on to  texas so it gives you an idea of kind of  the schedule and things that people are  talking about i think one of the most  valuable experiences for me this year's  national teacher is to be in close  contact with the business community  and hear about what they want our  students to be able to do and it's a  very different conversation often that  we're having in the education world you  know where we are in education right now  we talk a lot about curriculum and  testing and scores and standards very  interesting to have conversations with  people from the business world who have  a whole different set of expectations  for our students  and sometimes they'll say point blank  you know i don't care if you have a  straight a student who can get fives on  every ap test  if they can't also  step up and take a leadership role  work with self-confidence  be resilient and adaptable and  reflective  and independent and resilient and all  these other factors that they want our  students to be in fact i went to a  science facility in spokane washington  and i point blank asked the researchers  there i said if i could transport you  back to urbana high school to talk with  my students w