s those students saw  things that my other students never saw  that i never saw because they  they were so much more in tune with the  visual and just the hands-on nature that  they tapped into  aspects that i overlooked or that my  hearing students frankly didn't  appreciate so yes i would say especially  in the case where students are missing a  sense  that they're that much more in tune with  their other senses and that enables them  to experience to an even greater degree  you know another example would be a  student who  has severe dysgraphia  which means they have a lot of writing  processing difficulties those students  are often very verbal and they  participate extensively in class and  just by participating and hearing your  own voice and asking questions and  trying to process things verbally  brought them a whole different  understanding than students who were  just used to you know reading and  writing and reading and writing and  never heard it in their own voice  because they didn't participate to the  extent that they probably should have so  i think yes there's always some over  compensation  when there's a limitation and often that  that benefits the student  honestly i think the most exceptional  teachers are the ones who who really  reach beyond their content area and even  reach beyond the skills that we talk  about and really focus on the  interpersonal relationships with the  students and i know that sounds very  muddy and murky in our relationships you  know that's the key i mean it's the  teachers who can really find a way in a  class of 36 to make a student feel like  they're the only one in the class  i mean  that's really magical and it's very  tricky to pull off but there are  teachers who do it and i know that they  can because i often read  uh recommendations that students have  written about teachers and they'll often  say things like that you know that  teacher made me feel like the only  person in the class you know it kind of  goes beyond even caring it's just to  make somebody feel that special and to  be that in tune and very interesting  those students often say you know i  really don't know what it is about that  teacher that makes everybody want to  work so hard again it's that intangible  factor i mean there are just people who  are very gifted and really born to do  this job and that's that's really  special and it's something that can be  emulated you know again if we focus on  it you know if we focus on it and  realize the importance of relationships  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

