this is a production of cornell  university  thank you  great crowd so happy to be here this is  actually my fourth trip to new york this  year as national teacher of the year  i've been here all day at the university  spoke this morning actually at ithaca  college with ithaca college faculty and  also faculty from cornell this afternoon  had a chance to speak with education  majors from cornell and i'm here with  you tonight to give you a little bit  more broad perspective not just about my  background personally but what i've been  doing this year's national teacher of  the year and what the conversations are  that are going on in america right now  related to public education and sort of  how we can all be a part of that so i  really appreciate you coming out tonight  and i want to start by telling you just  a little bit about myself as was just  mentioned um i did go to princeton  university and  growing up i thought i always wanted to  be a teacher my mother's a retired  elementary school teacher and in general  i thought being a teacher would be a  really great thing to be  that career goal was sort of received in  a lukewarm fashion until i decided to  enroll at princeton university and then  the conversation really changed and then  the conversation became well you know  those who can do and those who can't  teach  and i was asked when i got to princeton  well  you know you came to princeton and all  you want to be is a teacher  and what a waste  and i know that some people here at  cornell can relate to that uh philosophy  and dr duff actually sitting up front  here is a fellow princetonian we  actually graduated from each other one  year apart and he thought it would be  nice for me to come here and kind of  share my perspective on how and why i  got into teaching despite  considerable social pressure not to do  so but it really all goes back to  this journey here i did start  volunteering  i decided when i was at princeton that  okay well maybe i should be something  more so i decided to be pre-med  and in general that career goal was very  well received so everybody else was  happy and i was plodding along planning  to be pre-med took the mcats did the  whole nine yards and i thought to myself  well you know if i'm not going to do  teaching as a career then by golly i'm  going to go volunteer  and i found myself in this classroom at  the new jersey school for the deaf  with these students who changed my life  they changed my life and people ask me  they said well you know what was it  about that environment what was it about  being with those students that made you  completely scrap your pre-med plans and  decide to go into teaching and for  anybody who's ever been in a classroom  it's easy to understand because there's  this energy  there's this interaction knowing that  you're making a difference every day i  just really wanted to be a part of that  and so i came from this environment  to where i am now  and this is a snapshot from my classroom  at urbana high school in frederick  county maryland  i've been teaching at the school  approximately a total of about 11 years  and i love this picture and i love to  tell people what i see in this picture i  look at this picture i see progress  because i see roughly an equal mix of  male and female students  and remember this is an ap chemistry  class  and that is not always the way it used  to be we are starting to close the  gender gap in some of our advanced  science subjects particularly in  chemistry and biology we have more work  to do in physics and math and  engineering but we're making progress i  look at the picture and i see some  diversity but not enough  because the diversity of my own  classroom still does not reflect the  diversity of my school and that's a  problem and our work is not finished  until it does  but students in my class who have 504  plans or ieps which means they have some  form of special needs over the years  i've accommodated students in my  classroom with everything from  asperger's syndrome to dyslexia  dysgraphia low vision and people tell me  sometimes that those students really  have no business being in my class  because it's an ap chemistry class  and that should mean something  and perhaps my expectations just aren't  high enough  i also look at this picture and i see at  least one face that's missing and it's  because that student is at the career  and technology center the vocational  student and somebody asked me well why  would a student in the vocational  program be in your ap chemistry class  that's a conflict  and my question is why is there only one  i mean with all the overlap between  taking knowledge and applying it which  is what i do in my chemistry classes  what they do in the vocational program  and aren't we really all teaching the  same things that we should be able to  make some more progress there  so in between in the middle of my career  i had the opportunity to teach at the  maryland school for the deaf and this is  a snapshot of a group of students that i  taught while i was there maryland school  for the deaf is a very interesting place  even though that maryland is a small  state it's a very diverse state and this  school draws students from all over the  state so students from our inner cities  students from our eastern shore the  beach areas students from the mountains  and everything in between and not just  students from different socioeconomic  backgrounds but students who have  different varying levels of hearing loss  so for example i taught some students  here who were born deaf and grew up with  families using american sign language i  taught other students who were not  diagnosed with their deafness till much  later in life so they came to me at the  high school level maybe not knowing any  sign language at all and of course at  this particular school all of the  instruction was through american sign  language we didn't use voice at all so  sometimes in my chemistry physics class  i would have a student who maybe was  growing up with spoken spanish in the  home had no idea about asl but yet we're  in my chemistry and physics class trying  to learn through asl so it's a very  complicated dynamic situation  so basically you know i've been hanging  out the better part of the past 15 years  you know teaching everybody's favorite  subject  and i can see that that's true here by  the show of hands about how many of you  this is really favorite subject and of  course i'm just kidding obviously  chemistry is not everybody's favorite  subject  but as educators as a teacher it's my  job to somehow bring this to everybody  and usually when people hear chemistry  they think of one of two things they  either think about the facts and the  formulas that they despise so much  or they think about the fun stuff they  think about the fun stuff and yeah  there's fun stuff in chemistry you know  my students love to do lab activities  and of course i try to engage them in  everything that i can when students come  into my classroom really every day they  want to know two things right they have  two questions for me first they want to  know  are we going to burn something today  that's number one and if we're not going  to burn something the question is are we  going to  blow something up  right that's what students want to do in  a chemistry class and so yes as a  teacher trying to engage my students i  try to bring them in any way that i can  i know that most of my students are  really into the harry potter series  and they've seen the movies and they see  that big green flu network do you know  what i'm talking about if you've seen  the movie and they know that's chemistry  and they say oh that's so cool you know  can we do that in your class and i say  sure we can do that and we set up little  green flu networks all over the counter  tops and then they see how they burn  green and they say oh you know can we  make them red and i say yeah we can do  that you know and then we turn them red  and then they want to know why and then  you can actually teach them something i  have students in my class who swear up  and down that they are not science  people you know i am not a science  person you are not going to turn me into  a science person they say i am a history  person and i said well that's fine you  know there's a lot of science in history  we talk about things like the hindenburg  explosion and all of my history buffs  can tell me all about that and they've  seen the video and they've read the  theories and then they say yeah that was  a really amazing explosion and then they  make the connection and there's the  explosion can we do that can we do that  in your classroom and i say yeah sure we  can do that we can take balloons and we  can fill them up with hydrogen gas and  in a carefully controlled situation  we can explode those little mini  hydrogen balloons so they can get a  sense for what that history was like  i've never met a student from anywhere  who doesn't want to set off an alcohol  cannon  it's just one of those universally  appealing things  and as teach as a teacher i would be  crazy not to tap into that but you know  it's not all about fun and games and  entertainment it's really so that our  students wind up with a serious look on  their face so that they get engaged  better yet to have this look of pride on  their face and i love this particular  picture because rochelle's lab work is  reflected in her goggles with that sense  of pride of having mastered something  really truly difficult and with  everything that we know about data these  days and there's a lot of talk in  education about data  data action research collect the data  what does it mean and that's all  important but i've learned over the  years and having starting in deaf  education that i get just as much  information out of looking at students  faces and that's something that i've  done all along but then last may 3rd i  found myself in a very unlikely place  for a public school teacher  with the president of the united states  to my right and the secretary of  education to my left standing with  the 55 state and territorial teachers of  the year  and knowing in that moment that i was no  longer a chemistry teacher hanging out  in my classroom blowing up stuff and  doing cool labs with my students  that i was now a spokesperson for public  education representing all teachers  early childhood teachers elementary  school teachers middle school teachers  high school teachers from all subject  areas and it would be my job for a year  to find the themes that connect us all  now as a little time out you might be  thinking  how exactly do you wind up in that place  i mean really how would you get chosen  for something like that what is the  process and i'll just make a long story  very very short but it actually started  at my school level the faculty that i  worked with in my school nominated me  put me on the ballot to apply for  teacher of the year and i told him i  didn't want to do it  i said i want to do it because it would  take too much time  away from my students because with any  process like this it's a process there's  a lot of essay writing there are  face-to-face interviews  and all the while you never think that  you're going to go very far anyway so i  did start the process because i was very  honored to be to be nominated and then i  was named teacher of the year at my  county level in frederick county and  maryland and then all of the county  teachers went to the state level and  then i went through another series of  evaluations at the state level and then  i was named the maryland teacher of the  year  and then once you're named your state  teacher of the year then all of those  application materials get sent to the  national committee and at that point the  game changes a little bit because at  that point if you're your state teacher  of the year it's pretty much a given  that everybody's fabulous in the  classroom everybody's doing amazing  things in the classroom and so from that  pool of 55 four finalists were selected  of which i was one we were all brought  into washington dc and in washington dc  we were put through a series of  evaluations that would simulate what we  would do as national teacher of the year  we had a classic interview where we had  to answer questions  we had to do a mock tv interview like a  good morning america we had to do a  press conference i had to do a keynote  speech we had to do a working networking  dinner  so different situations that would  evaluate our ability to be a  spokesperson which is what this year is  all about  people look at this picture and they  think that i have a direct line to the  president and the secretary of education  i do not  i do not everything that i do is by  invitation  and it's not a role where you are seated  with the policy makers that's not the  purpose of the year though i have had  some opportunities to interact with them  both that's certainly not the focus but  i have as i like to say  just been teaching in a larger classroom  i mean i've told myself all along that  the key word in national teacher of the  year is teacher you know i'm still a  teacher i'm always a teacher i've always  considered myself a teacher but i have a  much bigger classroom much bigger  audience this year if you look at the  map  everything that's shaded in hot pink  shows you where i have been as national  teacher of the year and many of those  states multiple visits i mean four  visits to new york three to texas three  to mississippi um history people please  forgive me geography folks but yes china  and japan floating around there  aimlessly in the ocean i have been to  china and japan obviously the map is not  to scale  also heading to singapore at the end of  may so that'll be three international  trips on my schedule you see that  arkansas is shaded orange instead of  pink that's because i took a virtual  trip to arkansas  i went to arkansas via skype  and i spoke with a group of teachers  about this large just through the  screens so pretty amazing how technology  is really bringing people together and  in yellow you see the states that i will  continue to visit and speak in even  after my term officially ends on june  1st and you say well  how come you didn't go to nevada and how  come you didn't go to minnesota remember  it's all by invitation i can't decide so  for example i'm still waiting on an  invitation to hawaii  i'm still waiting i'd love to go i think  i have a lot to say to educators there  but unfortunately that just hasn't come  through so it's all by invitation and to  some extent invitations uh there are  conflicts so for example i was scheduled  to go to south dakota but then the japan  trip came up so then the south dakota  event fell off my schedule so those  sorts of things happen  but basically as i go around the country  not only am i sharing what i'm doing and  what's going on in education i'm  learning and also i'm i'm answer asking  asking and answering a lot of questions  and what's interesting is as i travel a  lot of the questions that i get asked  particularly by the media are of a very  urgent  point-blank nature for example this is  the first question i was ever asked in a  phone interview  what's the secret  right  what's the secret  to teaching of course  as if i have that secret you know  written down on a little piece of paper  and folded up and in a lock box in my  classroom and if i would somehow just  let it out  then somehow everything would be okay  what's the secret here's another one why  can't we fix our schools  right why can't we just fix our schools  and by the way give it to me in 20  seconds or less because that's all you  have for this sound bite  because we do live in the sound bite  culture  and people are only willing to listen  for so long  and so  i've learned as every state teacher and  national teacher has learned that if you  have something to say to the media you  better say it quickly make your point  because otherwise the conversation is  going to change very quickly i love this  one this next question is yes yes yes i  understand that there are so many  challenges in education but  if you had a magic wand  i love that if you had a magic wand  and i got to a certain point where i had  been asked that question so many times  that i just flat out said in an  interview i don't have a magic wand no  one does  i mean that's the truthful answer  that is the truthful answer  it was on public television in fact  nobody has a magic wand  it's just not that simple and i know  that here in america we like that quick  fix we want that easy answer we want to  try to pretend that there's just one  thing that could make everything right  despite the diversity of our nation and  it's not just true but i understand that  these questions are all designed to get  at what the real issue is and the real  issue is how do we reach all students  that's what really weighs on everybody's  mind is the equity issue we we still  admit that in our country there are  pockets of excellence that's for sure  and then there are pockets of great need  and that there are huge disparities in  access and resources for students  sometimes the disparity is separated by  just a matter of miles  and and everybody's trying to figure out  why that continues and what we can do  about it but but it's it's a good  natured question i mean how do we reach  all students and you know now that i'm a  parent myself  this question is very very important to  me this is my little girl carly  and she's sitting with the boy she says  she's going to marry  and they are at their preschool  graduation  yeah their preschool graduation  and i noticed that some people in the  audience said oh like it's not cute and  that's great and some people laugh when  i say that  they're like preschool graduation like  are you serious like how silly to which  i say you know is it really that silly  is it really that silly if we're going  for equity here that from the youngest  possible age to have absolutely every  child wear that cap and gown and hold  that piece of paper so that they will  believe  that they too will be what we're calling  these days and you've probably heard the  term college and career ready  and i put that in quotes because we're  not going to debate it at the moment  but the point is that if we think about  our own children or a child very close  to us in our lives we know exactly what  that means  i know what it means for my daughter to  be college ready career ready life ready  world ready i know everything i want her  to be in my mind i don't need to see it  spelled out on a checklist i don't need  it to be in a manual or a curriculum or  a set of standards i know  what that is  and you know her little boyfriend i  don't know him very well  but  it's my job as an educator  to want for other people's children  what i want for my own  and as we work towards this equity that  we all desire so much i think if we can  just keep that very simple fact in mind  that moves us a huge step forward  so just to give you an idea of how that  conversation is playing out over the  country i'm going to take a week from my  schedule as national teacher and just  show you the different conversations  that i'm involved in and i can't  necessarily say that this week is  typical but it'll give you an idea of  some of the things that i've been doing  if we flash back to november um first i  was involved in a conference week  regarding educator effectiveness big  deal right now is about teachers and how  we're going to evaluate them and what  makes an effective teacher and what  makes an exceptional teacher and how do  we spell that all out in a form that's  somehow fair and perhaps could be linked  to compensation that's a big issue so my  day on monday november 14th started with  that issue and then i went down to  washington dc for congressional briefing  where we were talking about the ap  chemistry redesign curriculum is a big  issue in the in the discussion uh in the  country right now what are we going to  teach kids and even something uh with a  long strand standing tradition like the  ap programs they're even looking at  their curriculum and redesigning it and  trying to make it less facts more  application less memorization more  inquiry so the tests are changing  everything's changing really effective  immediately so i was weighing in on that  then i went to a women's heritage center  for a stem exhibit again equity is still  on everybody's minds we're trying to  figure out how do we get more women into  stem science technology engineering  mathematics how do we get more  individuals from minority backgrounds  into stem and so any event that kind of  highlights that is something that i  would be involved in you know we need to  celebrate what's going on in our public  schools so that we know what to  replicate and to emulate you turn on the  news on any given night and you hear a  lot of things that we're not doing right  in the public schools i mean that  dominates the conversation but there are  a lot of celebrations that go on and  it's it's very important because  otherwise we don't know what's truly  working so i found myself at the blue  ribbon schools  ceremony  with arne duncan celebrating great  things happening and then i found myself  later that day again celebrating  american education week because it's  really important that we get these  positive stories out in the news  i mean i i suggested in my national  teacher of the application i mean  imagine if the national news began each  evening with a segment titled highlights  in american public education  that actually celebrate what's really  working that's really going on that's  good in our public schools  not only would we be celebrating it but  we would know what to emulate and what  to replicate we don't know enough about  the great things that are really going  on as i travel the country people say oh  michelle have you heard about this going  on here have you heard about this  program and i say yeah no i i really  haven't  because we don't get that message out  there nearly enough so that's obviously  an issue of concern the next day i found  myself with students i found myself with  a little people  at a local elementary school i was  speaking to kindergartners first graders  all the way up to fifth graders and  their parents and what i found really  interesting at that student assembly and  you know there's a different situation  for me because remember i'm a high  school teacher but you know they  corralled them all into the cafeteria  like you probably remember when you were  in elementary school and everybody was  sitting in nice neat rows just like  their teachers told them with their legs  folded you know crisscross applesauce  and all that  and  they're all looking up at me  and you know when i grabbed the mic that  day  i actually hesitated for a minute before  i started speaking because i thought to  myself man  you know i better be good  because i've got the future sitting  there staring back at me  and whatever i say is going to stick in  their brains and i better be good  and i asked them some questions that day  now i'm not full enough to open up a  question to you know 500 kindergarteners  and have them all call out and screaming  but i taught him a little bit of sign  language i told him yes and i taught him  no and i asked the kindergartners i  asked them if they thought i looked like  a scientist  and over half of them said no  now tell me why  a five-year-old  would would tell me that i don't look  like a scientist i mean on what basis is  it because i'm a woman is it because i  have long hair and it's kind of curly  how do these images and these  perceptions gets ingrained in our  children so early  and little kids are very honest so  they'll tell you that so we really have  some work to do in our country with  perceptions starting from our very young  children and believing you know who  could be a scientist who could be a  college graduate you know who could be a  professional  you know we need to do a lot with  perceptions later that day i found  myself with my own little girl  at her parent day at her school and then  i caught a plane to go to phoenix and in  phoenix i went to a policy conference  when i go to a policy conference i'm  usually the only teacher there  i am the  teacher voice  the teacher voice  and in that particular situation at this  particular event we went on a tour of a  vocational school you know we have a lot  of students in our country who are on a  vocational track this is just a fact and  we have to serve their needs we also  have to understand our vocational  students because vocational students  also have college plans i got news for  you and often they have very big goals  and they have it all figured out because  they explain it to me they say yeah i'm  going to graduate from high school i'm  going to get this job i'm going to save  money then i'm going to go to college  then i'm going to go back and start my  own business so  it's very unfair of us  to put people on tracks and say well  you're either vocational or you're  academic because there's so much  crossover so i'd have a chance to go to  that school or chat on educator  effectiveness a little bit of networking  and then finally i was done  giving my own keynote and you might  think about this week that i just  described and somebody asked me once  they said what do you do when you get  sick  it's just not on the schedule it's just  it just doesn't happen you just um  fortunately i've had a very good year  but i've not gotten sick but that  particular week i got to go home on  sunday and you say well that's nice you  get at least you get to get home every 