ink  would bring us all together to  communicate more effectively  it's got to be that face to face you got  to have that face to face the technology  doesn't do it for us it really doesn't  it's a great question  absolutely and and the goal of the ap  class is not just to pass the test  you're absolutely right and again just  to use the example that i use for the  conversation i don't care how many your  kids pass the ap exam if they can't also  do this and that's a hard sell to  students sometimes especially in in  these economic times because students  and their parents will associate that  score with a gpa which gets associated  with a scholarship which gets you into  college so the conversation has really  changed in the past few years with even  more of an emphasis and a pressure from  students and parents to make sure that  teachers are helping them get that score  but let me let me turn my answer around  in a slightly different fashion because  you talked about the score issue  i accept students into my class who i  know their prognosis on the exam is not  assured  i mean it's just what i decided to do  many years ago i just decided to cast  the widest net  and i'll use one student as an example  she took my class it was a big risk you  know she didn't have all the skills she  needed and in the end  she did not pass the test she got she  got a two  and so if on that basis she is a failure  and so am i  right  but if you look at the bigger picture  and i think that's what you're getting  at the bigger picture that student  because of what she learned in my class  and again that focus on the critical  thinking and the communication all those  other skills  she carried all that with her to her  next ap science class and she was  successful and then she carried that on  to be the first in her family to go to  college and major in stem so when i say  take risks i mean you're right it's not  all about the test and if we go through  and we say oh well you're going to pass  the test and you're not then we're not  tapping a lot of the potential that  exists in our students especially in our  english language learners and students  who are for whatever reason  a bit behind in their education because  not all students follow the same  sequence so that's an excellent question  and is absolutely about more than the  test score but  that's a harder and harder sell these  days when so much is being tied to those  scores everything from gpas and  scholarships to teacher evaluation and  teacher pay  and in those conversations test scores  are not the sole indicator but but  they're being brought in as a big part  but what's really being discussed in the  educator effectiveness discussion is  multiple measures i mean yes the content  and test scores it's going to be a piece  it's going to be there the growth  however we're going to do it however  we're not going to get around it it's  going to be there and that's troublesome  because you know for a class like mine i  have a test it's kind of easy to do what  about all these untested areas like what  does that look like for for fourth grade  art or pe and how's that going to tie in  but it's going to be there one way or  the other they haven't figured it all  out yet but you know they're looking at  a lot of other different things  um in terms of how teachers you know  effectively manage their classes and how  they relate to students and all of this  but see i think the real sticking point  of the issue is that we're limiting  ourselves by using the term effective in  the first place  because i don't strive to be an  effective teacher  i strive to be an extraordinary teacher  and i have from the beginning and i  think the problem with the effectiveness  discussion is that we're trying to  figure out how to put it on a piece of  paper in a rubric  we're trying to code it because it's got  to be fair and understandable and  transparent and concrete and all that  when really what we want and i say the  collective we when you think about what  people want from their teachers they  don't just want effective they want that  magical teacher who can inspire and and  and you know again teach students all  these skills to see beyond themselves  all these intangible things that we want  so badly from our teachers that never  finds its way on to the checklist  and i think there are different ways we  can go about that i mean for example in  my own experience as an educator i've  never been asked until i started the  teacher of the year program what do you  do  that you consider special what do you do  to connect with kids i've been asked  what committees i've been on and what  courses i've taken for my professional  development but nobody's ever nobody  ever really asked me  why why do how do you get 100 kids in ap  chemistry  what is your philosophy what do you say  what you do and i think we need to take  it to that next level whatever this next  educator effectiveness is going to look  like has to account for the intangibles  because otherwise we will never have  that that higher model of what we want  our teachers to be so the people even  know what to strive for that we're  always kind of limited by something that  goes on paper and that's kind of what  i'm trying to push in the discussion but  it's it's far from over i mean most  states are just still waiting through  what they're going to do and what  they're going to pilot and it's it's all  very  um  tentative and still very much in the  works  that's a great question  sure absolutely i think i saw that right  away when i worked with the students you  know at the school for the deaf i think  that students who are in particular  missing one sense are that much more in  tune with their other senses and i  noticed for example i taught five years  at urbana high school hearing students  okay and they reacted to things in a  certain way in the lab  when i went to the school for the deaf  doing the same labs those students saw  things that my other students never saw  that i never saw because they  they were so much more in tune with the  visual and just the hands-on nature that  they tapped into  aspects that i overlooked or that my  hearing students frankly didn't  appreciate so yes i would say especially  in the case where students are missing a  sense  that they're that much more in tune with  their other senses and that enables them  to experience to an even greater degree  you know another example would be a  student who  has severe dysgraphia  which means they have a lot of writing  processing difficulties those students  are often very verbal and they  participate extensively in class and  just by participating and hearing your  own voice and asking questions and  trying to process things verbally  brought them a whole different  understanding than students who were  just used to you know reading and  writing and reading and writing and  never heard it in their own voice  because they didn't participate to the  extent that they probably should have so  i think yes there's always some over  compensation  when there's a limitation and often that  that benefits the student  honestly i think the most exceptional  teachers are the ones who who really  reach beyond their content area and even  reach beyond the skills that we talk  about and really focus on the  interpersonal relationships with the  students and i know that sounds very  muddy and murky in our relationships you  know that's the key i mean it's the  teachers who can really find a way in a  class of 36 to make a student feel like  they're the only one in the class  i mean  that's really magical and it's very  tricky to pull off but there are  teachers who do it and i know that they  can because i often read  uh recommendations that students have  written about teachers and they'll often  say things like that you know that  teacher made me feel like the only  person in the class you know it kind of  goes beyond even caring it's just to  make somebody feel that special and to  be that in tune and very interesting  those students often say you know i  really don't know what it is about that  teacher that makes everybody want to  work so hard again it's that intangible  factor i mean there are just people who  are very gifted and really born to do  this job and that's that's really  special and it's something that can be  emulated you know again if we focus on  it you know if we focus on it and  realize the importance of relationships  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu
 

