e teacher and  national teacher has learned that if you  have something to say to the media you  better say it quickly make your point  because otherwise the conversation is  going to change very quickly i love this  one this next question is yes yes yes i  understand that there are so many  challenges in education but  if you had a magic wand  i love that if you had a magic wand  and i got to a certain point where i had  been asked that question so many times  that i just flat out said in an  interview i don't have a magic wand no  one does  i mean that's the truthful answer  that is the truthful answer  it was on public television in fact  nobody has a magic wand  it's just not that simple and i know  that here in america we like that quick  fix we want that easy answer we want to  try to pretend that there's just one  thing that could make everything right  despite the diversity of our nation and  it's not just true but i understand that  these questions are all designed to get  at what the real issue is and the real  issue is how do we reach all students  that's what really weighs on everybody's  mind is the equity issue we we still  admit that in our country there are  pockets of excellence that's for sure  and then there are pockets of great need  and that there are huge disparities in  access and resources for students  sometimes the disparity is separated by  just a matter of miles  and and everybody's trying to figure out  why that continues and what we can do  about it but but it's it's a good  natured question i mean how do we reach  all students and you know now that i'm a  parent myself  this question is very very important to  me this is my little girl carly  and she's sitting with the boy she says  she's going to marry  and they are at their preschool  graduation  yeah their preschool graduation  and i noticed that some people in the  audience said oh like it's not cute and  that's great and some people laugh when  i say that  they're like preschool graduation like  are you serious like how silly to which  i say you know is it really that silly  is it really that silly if we're going  for equity here that from the youngest  possible age to have absolutely every  child wear that cap and gown and hold  that piece of paper so that they will  believe  that they too will be what we're calling  these days and you've probably heard the  term college and career ready  and i put that in quotes because we're  not going to debate it at the moment  but the point is that if we think about  our own children or a child very close  to us in our lives we know exactly what  that means  i know what it means for my daughter to  be college ready career ready life ready  world ready i know everything i want her  to be in my mind i don't need to see it  spelled out on a checklist i don't need  it to be in a manual or a curriculum or  a set of standards i know  what that is  and you know her little boyfriend i  don't know him very well  but  it's my job as an educator  to want for other people's children  what i want for my own  and as we work towards this equity that  we all desire so much i think if we can  just keep that very simple fact in mind  that moves us a huge step forward  so just to give you an idea of how that  conversation is playing out over the  country i'm going to take a week from my  schedule as national teacher and just  show you the different conversations  that i'm involved in and i can't  necessarily say that this week is  typical but it'll give you an idea of  some of the things that i've been doing  if we flash back to november um first i  was involved in a conference week  regarding educator effectiveness big  deal right now is about teachers and how  we're going to evaluate them and what  makes an effective teacher and what  makes an exceptional teacher and how do  we spell that all out in a form that's  somehow fair and perhaps could be linked  to compensation that's a big issue so my  day on monday november