nted to talk about was how do we  get stem into the elementary school  years  realizing that high school is too late  to start getting students interested in  serious science and what were we doing  to bring science education to the  elementary school levels and of course  that's a struggle for us here  in a lot of areas  even in my daughter's elementary school  science is still pushed to the back  burner because we have this focus on  testing and common core standards of  course are coming on and that's giving  you know even more focus to english and  of course math and so when do you do the  science but yet everybody wants all  these scientists for the future how are  we going to create them if we don't  start from the youngest possible age and  in japan they realize that that's an  issue as well i took a little japanese  in college so i had a little bit of  working knowledge  they welcomed me warmly with the  traditional tea ceremonies and all of  that so that was really nice had a  chance to visit a broad range of schools  in japan they were very very eager to  show me everything took me to something  they called a super science school a  super science school and this is one of  these highly selective schools that has  absolutely everything all the latest  technology  all of the you know on-site research  facility and when we drove away from  that school my host said to me i want  you to understand that that is the  exception  that that is the exception and then i  visited many other schools that you know  would be comparable to many of the  schools that we have here you know good  schools but not necessarily the latest  of everything they also took me to a  school that served students with special  needs and students that have issues with  truancy and trying to figure out how to  include those students in  the system and i think probably the most  people say what's been the highlight of  your year what's been the moment that's  been the most impactful i would have to  say it would be the end of my trip where  i went to onagawa city japan now because  this is such a large screen i think  you're going to be able to see what i'm  getting at and we'll zoom in in a minute  but they took me to a region that had  been devastated by the tsunami  and at the very beginning of the week  you know i had a full schedule of events  you know i had to meet with the minister  and i had to give presentations and meet  with teachers and all that they said at  the end of the week the very last thing  they said would you teach a class  to our students  they said we have this group of students  uh we'll bring in with some sixth  graders they have lost everything will  you come teach a class and so of course  somebody says teach class and i haven't  taught for many many months and so i  start thinking i start thinking about  the labs we can do i start thinking  about what i'm going to need and the  curriculum i said you know what do you  have and they said nothing  so we don't have anything  but they still wanted me to create this  magical amazing moment for their  students and again just to give you a  little bit of a picture if we zoom in a  little bit you know this was still the  situation when i was there in october  after the tsunami hit in march i mean  everything just completely pushed off  into piles it really cannot be captured  uh by a camera but where there were  buildings standing still debris just  strewn all over the place everywhere um  you know people's personal belongings  just still scattered you know amidst the  streets and i was led around by this  this wonderful man a teacher who's  actually standing on the site where his  home was washed away  is his home was standing where the  yellow flowers are and he's pointing up  to the school and of course the school  being high on the hill  the lives of the students and the  teachers were spared because the tsunami  hit  at 3 30 in the afternoon  while many of their parents and the 