or me that i  guess if i had to pick one out for the  year that just really set in is really  powerful and and not many days go by i  don't think about these students  on the other side of the world  with with nothing  i mean they're living in makeshift  trailers and their teachers are living  in trailers and they're trying to go  about everyday life and do everything  that we're trying to do and master all  these skills and all this curriculum  with absolutely nothing and and it's a  very powerful moment  you  know i get caught up in a lot of the  debates  on education  one of the debates that i get caught up  is you know i haven't students change  you know you say students are students  everywhere but these are different  students now they're different people  these are digital natives you know  they've all grown up with all this  technology and somehow that makes them  different and you know that's a long  debate and we can certainly get into it  and as i said to students today i mean  technology has done amazing things for  all of us and we'll continue to do so i  embrace technology i've seen it do  amazing things for students with special  needs but the bottom line is when you  think about everything that we want for  our students i mean a computer cannot  talk a student out of dropping a class  and a piece of technology cannot inspire  a child to be the first in their family  to go to college  and i don't know of any gadget that can  teach a student to see beyond himself  so yes there's technology  there are tools of technology but there  are teachers and there are people and  that's where the real power is it's it's  a people enterprise and we need as much  power people power as we can get  you know as i said people ask me you  know why did you even do this you know  you came to princeton and all you're  going to be as a teacher and i know that  that's not unique to princeton that that  that  message  is conveyed on many many college  campuses and universities across the  country that if you're going to be  somebody you've got to be something  other than a teacher had the opportunity  to go back to princeton  and visit the school where i originally  started to volunteer  and go back to my roots and this is a  photograph taken almost 20 years ago  and  i actually connected with my experience  from japan because when i was  volunteering at the new jersey school  for the deaf we really didn't have what  we needed either and as if you look at  the countertops we're doing a physics  lab and we're using hot wheels cars and  we're using track to try to do force  experiments and acceleration because as  teachers that's what we do we do what we  need to do and we scrounge for materials  when we need them had a chance in at  that visit in february to reunite with  my cooperating teacher we were  reminiscing about how we used to to beg  for materials this was back in the day i  say back in the day says 1994. this was  back in the day  i'm i'm actually if you're really good  i'm old enough to say back in my day you  know this was when film for cameras used  to come in those containers do you know  what i'm talking about some of you don't  even know what i'm talking about there's  little black containers with the gray  lids and i remember that we used to go  and we used to we used to beg these  stores that developed film and we'd say  hey before you don't just throw those  things out can you donate those to the  school because if we could have those  little containers we could change the  mass of the pennies inside we can hang  them on strings and do pendulum  experiments i mean you know what i was  doing back in 1994 wasn't so different  than what i was doing in japan in the  tsunami region just just making do and  trying to bring the best possible  education that we could to the kids it's  interesting we brought over some gate  timers at the time the wooden  contraptions you see on the wall there  and you see the surprise look at 