per grading and it  wasn't the meetings and it wasn't the  curriculum and the planning lessons it  was all these things that you have to be  all day every day everything from  passionate to creative to engaging to  dedicated and fearless and you're  pouring your energy into other people  all the time and you're left at the end  of the day with absolutely nothing  and you're just completely drained and  all you want to do is go to bed and take  a nap and you wonder how you're going to  get up the next day and do it all again  but then you think about the students  and you know that they're going to feed  that energy right back into you and and  that's what gets you out of bed to do it  all again  you know i think my biggest pet peeve as  i've traveled is to hear a teacher say  i'm just a teacher  really  i'm just a teacher  and again we don't have time at the  moment to go into all the things that a  teacher has to be but i'm just a teacher  actually it's i've asked teachers to  make just a simple change to that  statement i mean i am a teacher  i am a teacher  and it's it's quite a difficult sell  sometimes to explain what that's all  about and again people can only listen  for so long and everybody wants it in a  sound bite well in conclusion here  there's this very interesting contest  where they asked  people to send in in six words  what it means to be a teacher  again sound bite culture right six words  and i went to the website and i looked  at all the different entries and then  some of them were very uh nifty and  impressive and catchy for example one of  them said teachers hold ladders that  students climb there's your six words  teachers hold ladders that students  climb that's a nice image there was  another one that was a little bit more  sciency that said  teachers point out rockets no i'm sorry  point out the stars provide rockets  okay again i like the scientific imagery  but i was reading through the list and  there was one that really made me sit  back in my chair that i thought yes  this is what describes in six words  teachers and and nearly everybody  engaged in education  attributed into somebody named amanda w  in nevada  and i really appreciate her words  yes  selflessly dedicated to someone else's  success  that is what it means to be a teacher  selflessly dedicated to someone else's  success  and for me  there's been a lot of those someone  else's  i knew a long time ago that i was going  to need something to remind me why i  decided to become a teacher first place  because it's a very very demanding job  there are days it's extremely difficult  for me to get out of bed at 4 45 in the  morning which is the time that i have to  get up to make it to my school on time  to teach  and there are nights when i listen to  the news and i hear the conversation  about education how negative it's all  become and and i just want to run from  it and that's just the honest truth  but then i get to school and i know that  this is waiting for me  and i decided a long time ago not to  cover my bulletin boards with messages  or calendars or postings but just to  cover my bulletin boards with the faces  of students  so that i would always remember why i  decided to be a teacher and what i'm  doing there and i go to my classroom and  i see pictures of students from the  school for the deaf who won their  academic world championship i see  pictures of students who have gone on to  be scientists and engineers i see  pictures of students who have gone on to  be teachers i see pictures of students  who have given their lives for our  country  and it's been a great honor for me as i  have traveled this year to represent  students and represent teachers and to  really spread the message that  you know public education keyword is  public it's for everybody and it's a  human business and everybody has  something to contribute i mean you don't  have to be the national teacher of the  year to take an interest in a child's  education  right you don't have to be a chemistry  teacher to get involved with your local  community or whatever is going on with  stem or science  i mean everybody has the ability to  inspire students  to see beyond themselves  that it's really a human business and  it's that human factor that makes all  the difference thank you so much  for allowing me to be here tonight  absolutely i mean my first year my first  full year of teaching was actually my  public  school classroom  and you're right you're probably going  to have it's very likely you'll have a  difficult situation you might have  between 30 and 40 students in class you  might be pushing a cart  you might not have enough books enough  desks i mean again there's this  when you start off we all have this  starry-eyed image of going into the  classroom and changing the world and how  magical it's all going to be and then  you realize you're dealing with people  and you're dealing with difficult  situations and often not the resources  that you need  the first year of teaching is tough but  the 15th year of teaching is tough too  in a different way  i think the hardest part when you step  into that classroom for the first time  is realizing that despite public  perception this is not an easy job  and it will consume you 24 hours a day  because not only does it take that long  just to get the lessons planned and the  materials together and and you know make  sure you're grading the work and  analyzing your data and attending  whatever meeting you have to do and  maybe you're in extracurricular  activities and you're trying to keep up  with latest technology and curriculum  and all that it's 24 7.  not to mention the fact that you care  about kids you know so you're laying  awake at night like thinking about  johnny or bobby or thinking about you  know what you did or said or didn't  and it's all  consuming and a lot of people  bolt  after the first few years because it's  not what they expected  and i think it's really important to  understand that as you embark on your  first classroom is hey it's not gonna be  easy  and and b if you're the type of person  that really loves a challenge  and  not just a challenge but something  that's dynamic every day is different  every student is different nothing that  you do will ever work the same way twice  i mean i'm a 15-year teacher and i'm  still every day looking at every class  as a new class and every kid's a  different kid and if you love that sort  of complexity  then you will love teaching  and the energy that you put into it will  come back to you and that's what will  feed you and keep you going you know  it's like i say to my budding scientists  i said if you're the type of person who  wants immediate answers you probably  shouldn't be in research science because  there are people who work their entire  lives on a scientific problem and  just take one step towards the overall  picture because they love the process  and they love the process and that's why  they do it i think it's the same thing  in teaching  you have to be able to embrace the chaos  you embrace the chaos and you realize  that you are making a difference right i  mean teachers change lives  they really do not every day is a kodak  moment  and sometimes uh those those moments are  few and far between  but you're changing lives and and it's a  great honor to change a life even one  life  and as a teacher you have the  opportunity to change so many  so keep that in mind  and put that message on your desk  selflessly dedicated to someone else's  success and that will keep you going  through that first year great question  and good luck to you  that's a fantastic point i mean  everybody's involved everybody has a  role and thank goodness for that because  kids need all the support that they can  get and you brought up parents i mean  talking about parents that's like a  whole other hour-long discussion it  really is and i mean parents are the  most important people in a child's life  and that's just a fact and nobody has  greater power than the parent to  positively affect a child's academic  success and one of the things that i  talk about with new teachers is that  it's important to sort out like parent  contact and parent involvement like  these days a lot of times we think well  you know i posted a message on my web  page so i contacted the parent and and  that's good enough that's not good  enough we have to involve our parents  you're right because they play a big  role for me it's very interesting  because a lot of parents think they  can't be involved with what i do because  i teach ap chemistry and a lot of  parents either say well you know i never  took chemistry i took chemistry but i  hated it i took chemistry 25 years ago  and they feel that they can't be  involved so it's my job as a teacher yes  to bring them in meaning one of the  things i try to do is i try to give my  parents a list of ways they can be  involved like a menu of options for how  they can support their child and it's  the same thing with community members i  think as as teachers  we need to  do a better job of reaching out and  telling people you know here's what we  need from our parents here's what we  need for a community because often  people want to help and they just don't  know how because it hasn't been spelled  out for them obviously your building  administrator has a huge impact i've  been very fortunate to work with  administrators who have encouraged me to  take risks  i mean that's how i get you know 100  kids into ap chemistry because i've been  allowed to take risks that's how i went  to the maryland school for the deaf and  brought them ap chemistry for the first  time because there was a principal who  said let's forge ahead let's do it be  the flagship program  you know so yes the administrator has an  absolutely important role everybody has  a role to play you're right and and to  be honest  even with technology which you think  would bring us all together to  communicate more effectively  it's got to be that face to face you got  to have that face to face the technology  doesn't do it for us it really doesn't  it's a great question  absolutely and and the goal of the ap  class is not just to pass the test  you're absolutely right and again just  to use the example that i use for the  conversation i don't care how many your  kids pass the ap exam if they can't also  do this and that's a hard sell to  students sometimes especially in in  these economic times because students  and their parents will associate that  score with a gpa which gets associated  with a scholarship which gets you into  college so the conversation has really  changed in the past few years with even  more of an emphasis and a pressure from  students and parents to make sure that  teachers are helping them get that score  but let me let me turn my answer around  in a slightly different fashion because  you talked about the score issue  i accept students into my class who i  know their prognosis on the exam is not  assured  i mean it's just what i decided to do  many years ago i just decided to cast  the widest net  and i'll use one student as an example  she took my class it was a big risk you  know she didn't have all the skills she  needed and in the end  she did not pass the test she got she  got a two  and so if on that basis she is a failure  and so am i  right  but if you look at the bigger picture  and i think that's what you're getting  at the bigger picture that student  because of what she learned in my class  and again that focus on the critical  thinking and the communication all those  other skills  she carried all that with her to her  next ap science class and she was  successful and then she carried that on  to be the first in her family to go to  college and major in stem so when i say  take risks i mean you're right it's not  all about the test and if we go through  and we say oh well you're going to pass  the test and you're not then we're not  tapping a lot of the potential that  exists in our students especially in our  english language learners and students  who are for whatever reason  a bit behind in their education because  not all students follow the same  sequence so that's an excellent question  and is absolutely about more than the  test score but  that's a harder and harder sell these  days when so much is being tied to those  scores everything from gpas and  scholarships to teacher evaluation and  teacher pay  and in those conversations test scores  are not the sole indicator but but  they're being brought in as a big part  but what's really being discussed in the  educator effectiveness discussion is  multiple measures i mean yes the content  and test scores it's going to be a piece  it's going to be there the growth  however we're going to do it however  we're not going to get around it it's  going to be there and that's troublesome  because you know for a class like mine i  have a test it's kind of easy to do what  about all these untested areas like what  does that look like for for fourth grade  art or pe and how's that going to tie in  but it's going to be there one way or  the other they haven't figured it all  out yet but you know they're looking at  a lot of other different things  um in terms of how teachers you know  effectively manage their classes and how  they relate to students and all of this  but see i think the real sticking point  of the issue is that we're limiting  ourselves by using the term effective in  the first place  because i don't strive to be an  effective teacher  i strive to be an extraordinary teacher  and i have from the beginning and i  think the problem with the effectiveness  discussion is that we're trying to  figure out how to put it on a piece of  paper in a rubric  we're trying to code it because it's got  to be fair and understandable and  transparent and concrete and all that  when really what we want and i say the  collective we when you think about what  people want from their teachers they  don't just want effective they want that  magical teacher who can inspire and and  and you know again teach students all  these skills to see beyond themselves  all these intangible things that we want  so badly from our teachers that never  finds its way on to the checklist  and i think there are different ways we  can go about that i mean for example in  my own experience as an educator i've  never been asked until i started the  teacher of the year program what do you  do  that you consider special what do you do  to connect with kids i've been asked  what committees i've been on and what  courses i've taken for my professional  development but nobody's ever nobody  ever really asked me  why why do how do you get 100 kids in ap  chemistry  what is your philosophy what do you say  what you do and i think we need to take  it to that next level whatever this next  educator effectiveness is going to look  like has to account for the intangibles  because otherwise we will never have  that that higher model of what we want  our teachers to be so the people even  know what to strive for that we're  always kind of limited by something that  goes on paper and that's kind of what  i'm trying to push in the discussion but  it's it's far from over i mean most  states are just still waiting through  what they're going to do and what  they're going to pilot and it's it's all  very  um  tentative and still very much in the  works  that's a great question  sure absolutely i think i saw that right  away when i worked with the students you  know at the school for the deaf i think  that students who are in particular  missing one sense are that much more in  tune with their other senses and i  noticed for example i taught five years  at urbana high school hearing students  okay and they reacted to things in a  certain way in the lab  when i went to the school for the deaf  doing the same labs those students saw  things that my other students never saw  that i never saw because they  they were so much more in tune with the  visual and just the hands-on nature that  they tapped into  aspects that i overlooked or that my  hearing students frankly didn't  appreciate so yes i would say especially  in the case where students are missing a  sense  that they're that much more in tune with  their other senses and that enables them  to experience to an even greater degree  you know another example would be a  student who  has severe dysgraphia  which means they have a lot of writing  processing difficulties those students  are often very verbal and they  participate extensively in class and  just by participating and hearing your  own voice and asking questions and  trying to process things verbally  brought them a whole different  understanding than students who were  just used to you know reading and  writing and reading and writing and  never heard it in their own voice  because they didn't participate to the  extent that they probably should have so  i think yes there's always some over  compensation  when there's a limitation and often that  that benefits the student  honestly i think the most exceptional  teachers are the ones who who really  reach beyond their content area and even  reach beyond the skills that we talk  about and really focus on the  interpersonal relationships with the  students and i know that sounds very  muddy and murky in our relationships you  know that's the key i mean it's the  teachers who can really find a way in a  class of 36 to make a student feel like  they're the only one in the class  i mean  that's really magical and it's very  tricky to pull off but there are  teachers who do it and i know that they  can because i often read  uh recommendations that students have  written about teachers and they'll often  say things like that you know that  teacher made me feel like the only  person in the class you know it kind of  goes beyond even caring it's just to  make somebody feel that special and to  be that in tune and very interesting  those students often say you know i  really don't know what it is about that  teacher that makes everybody want to  work so hard again it's that intangible  factor i mean there are just people who  are very gifted and really born to do  this job and that's that's really  special and it's something that can be  emulated you know again if we focus on  it you know if we focus on it and  realize the importance of relationships  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


